# Citation metadata. GitHub renders this into a "Cite this repository" button
# with ready-to-paste BibTeX — which is the difference between someone meaning
# to cite the tool and actually doing it.
#
# ✅ COMPLETE as of 2026-08-23. `repository-code` was filled once the repository
#    existed; `doi` once Zenodo reserved one. Neither was ever committed as a
#    placeholder — a CITATION.cff with a dead DOI is worse than one with none.
cff-version: 1.2.0
title: "DL-TerrainDiversity"
message: "If you use this software, please cite it using these metadata."
type: software
authors:
  - family-names: Ihle
    given-names: Marc-Eduard
    affiliation: "Digital Landscapes"
    # ⚠️ STANDING RULE: the affiliation for a Digital Landscapes tool is
    # Digital Landscapes, never a university. The instrument is owned
    # independently; see NOTICE.
abstract: >-
  A Terrain Design Information Model (TDIM) for landscape architecture: the
  ground is held as a model the designer works with, from which the drawings —
  grading plan, chainage sections, cut/fill isopach, slope-class and drainage
  plans, GIS rasters — are derived rather than drawn. A model of terrain is a
  DTM; the design is the distinction. It couples a design gesture to its
  ecological consequence in one loop — terrain metrics answered in the same
  frame as the hand, hydrological derivatives within about thirty milliseconds,
  and radiation, shelter and landform on settling — and translates the resulting
  abiotic conditions into a modelled species assemblage. Built to test a single
  claim: that the relief which differentiates habitat falls below the resolution
  of national terrain and soil data, and therefore has to be designed and
  specified rather than surveyed. Runs in a browser with no installation and no
  build step — its one runtime library is vendored, so there is nothing to
  fetch; exports GIS-grade rasters, meshes, polygons and a full provenance
  record, each carrying the coordinate system its source declared. It remains a
  terrain analysis instrument, not a prediction.
keywords:
  - terrain design information model
  - TDIM
  - landscape architecture
  - digital terrain analysis
  - geomorphometry
  - geodiversity
  - habitat suitability
  - earthworks
  - cut and fill
  - grading plan
  - post-industrial landscape
license: Apache-2.0
repository-code: "https://github.com/DLandscapes/dl-terrain-diversity"
url: "https://digital-landscapes.com/"
version: "0.12.0"
date-released: "2026-08-23"
# ⚠️ THE VERSION DOI, RESERVED BEFORE THE DEPOSIT WAS PUBLISHED. That order is
#    the whole point: reserving first meant this line could be committed and
#    TAGGED, so the archive Zenodo keeps contains a citation file that knows
#    its own identifier — rather than one describing a DOI minted after the
#    fact, which is what the automatic GitHub integration would have produced.
doi: "10.5281/zenodo.22067911"
references:
  # ⚠️ THE METHOD'S OWN PRIOR PUBLICATION, AND THE DATE IS THE POINT. TDIM was
  # defined here, on Digital Landscapes' own site, on 23 April 2018 — under an
  # Innsbruck affiliation and four years before any UiT involvement. Carrying it
  # in the citation metadata makes the lineage machine-readable rather than a
  # claim in a README.
  - type: blog
    title: "TDIM Analysis from Case Study Festvåg — digital Terrain Design Information Model"
    authors:
      - family-names: Ihle
        given-names: Marc-Eduard
        affiliation: "University of Innsbruck, Institute of Experimental Architecture"
    url: "https://digital-landscapes.com/2018/04/23/digital-terrain-design-information-model/"
    year: 2018
    month: 4
    notes: >-
      Defines the Terrain Design Information Model. Multiscale integration of UAV
      survey with Kartverket open geodata; partners Grid-IT and Kartverket. A
      companion study applies the model on Svalbard.
  - type: conference-paper
    title: >-
      Digital Terrain Analysis as an Interface of Care: Revealing Morphological
      Complexity to Support Ecological Diversity
    # ⚠️ NO affiliation FIELD, DELIBERATELY. Standing rule: the affiliation for
    # every Digital Landscapes tool is Digital Landscapes, never a university.
    # The field is OMITTED rather than reassigned — this entry describes a
    # conference paper, and asserting a different affiliation than the one that
    # paper carries would make the citation wrong. Dropping the field keeps the
    # reference accurate (author, title, conference, venue, year all stand) and
    # states no institution at all.
    authors:
      - family-names: Ihle
        given-names: Marc-Eduard
    conference:
      name: >-
        ECLAS 2026 — Take Care: Planetary Landscape Architecture
    location:
      name: "TU Delft, Delft, Netherlands"
    year: 2026
    month: 9
